{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059961 :question/math-academy-id "q-10248" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ on $M?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3+(3)(-1) & -1+(3)(2) & 9+(3)(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059962 :question/math-academy-id "q-10250" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237897 :question/math-academy-id "q-10244" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265796 :question/math-academy-id "q-130006" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265809 :question/math-academy-id "q-130064" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the second row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0+(-2)(5) & 4+(-2)(2) & 7+(-2)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(1)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1+(1)(5) & 3+(1)(2) & 0+(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265810 :question/math-academy-id "q-130065" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the third row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $5$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1+(3)(2) & 2+(3)(0) & 1+(3)(-1) \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9+(5)(2) & 0+(5)(0) & -4+(5)(-1) \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287981 :question/math-academy-id "q-20239" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-3$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3+(-3)(1) & -1+(-3)(2) & 1+(-3)(1) \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386014 :question/math-academy-id "q-10245" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $B?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $4$ times the first row to the second row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ -4+(4)(1) & 12+(4)(3) & 3+(4)(6) \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 24 & 27 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 24 & 27 \\\\ -1+1 & 2+3 & 4+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 24 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388062 :question/math-academy-id "q-130010" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the second row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 1+(3)(9) & -3+(3)(6) & -2+(3)(-7) \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 28 & 15 & -23 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 28+(-1)(-2) & 15+(-1)(4) & -23+(-1)(-3) \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & -7 \\\\ 30 & 11 & -20 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525161 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-3$ times the third row to the first row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}3+(-3)(1) & -7+(-3)(-1) & 3+(-3)(0) \\\\ -1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525176 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 1+(-4)(-3) & -4+(-4)(1) \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 13 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525177 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-5{R}_{3}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-5{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-5$ times the third row to the first row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-5{R}_{3} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}5+(-5)(1) & 3+(-5)(0) & 6+(-5)(3) \\\\ 1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525181 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-3$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 3+(-3)(1) & 0+(-3)(-8) & 0+(-3)(6) \\\\ 1 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 24 & -18 \\\\ 1 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525191 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}$ on $M?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 3+(3)(-1) & -2+(3)(5) & 7+(3)(3) \\\\ -5 & -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\sim\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 16 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059581 :question/math-academy-id "e-4694" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059956 :question/math-academy-id "q-10217" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ on $M?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5+(-1)(5) & -1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(-1) & 1+(-1)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059963 :question/math-academy-id "q-10252" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$$\nA = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\n$$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{1}:=2R_{1}$ on $A$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265811 :question/math-academy-id "q-130070" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265812 :question/math-academy-id "q-130072" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $V,$ given by\n\n$$\nV=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $V?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ means that we need to swap the first and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4+(-1)(-1) & 4+(-1)(9) & 6+(-1)(-2) & 7+(-1)(6) \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341857 :question/math-academy-id "q-72763" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ on $M?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1+(-1)(1) & -1+(-1)(0) & 1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(3)\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341863 :question/math-academy-id "q-72786" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $U,$ given by\n\n$$\nU=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $U?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}$ means that we need to multiply the second row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0+0 & -1+1 & -3+0 & 4+3\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388069 :question/math-academy-id "q-130071" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $-\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{3}:=(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 6 & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (-3) & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 9 & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 0\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393222 :question/math-academy-id "q-72757" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ on $T?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $2$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2+(2)(1) & -2+(2)(3) & 1+(2)(2)\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393229 :question/math-academy-id "q-72760" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ on $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1+(-1)(1) & 5+(-1)(3) & -1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(1) \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393236 :question/math-academy-id "q-72781" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $N,$ given by\n\n$$\nN=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ on $N?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2+(-2)(1) & 1+(-2)(-2) & 2+(-2)(1) & 3+(-2)(-1) \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409514 :question/math-academy-id "q-72785" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $V,$ given by\n\n$$\nV=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $V?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ means that we need to swap the second and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1+(-1)(1) & -1+(-1)(0) & 0+(-1)(0) & 3+(-1)(2) \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059582 :question/math-academy-id "e-4695" :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060199 :question/math-academy-id "q-20240" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060203 :question/math-academy-id "q-20389" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $Y$, where\n$$\nX = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nY = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265820 :question/math-academy-id "q-130087" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265822 :question/math-academy-id "q-130095" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix  $C$ into the matrix $D$, where \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265824 :question/math-academy-id "q-130100" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $E$ into the matrix $F$, where\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $2$nd row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $2$nd and the $3$rd row, we notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265825 :question/math-academy-id "q-130103" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287989 :question/math-academy-id "q-20387" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $S$ into the matrix $T$, where\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that changes the $1$st  row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341877 :question/math-academy-id "q-72850" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $U$ into the matrix $V$, where\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $1$st row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $1$st and the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341878 :question/math-academy-id "q-72854" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341879 :question/math-academy-id "q-72856" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $K$ into the matrix $L$, where\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059583 :question/math-academy-id "e-4696" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060202 :question/math-academy-id "q-20388" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060204 :question/math-academy-id "q-20392" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\nX = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes $a_{21} = 0$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265873 :question/math-academy-id "q-130283" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{13}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265874 :question/math-academy-id "q-130285" :question/problem "Given \n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{32}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265876 :question/math-academy-id "q-130301" :question/problem "Given \n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\n which of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{23}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265877 :question/math-academy-id "q-130304" :question/problem "Given \n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{32}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -20 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287982 :question/math-academy-id "q-20241" :question/problem "Given \n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287988 :question/math-academy-id "q-20386" :question/problem "Given \n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\n which of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{31}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341880 :question/math-academy-id "q-72857" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation    \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 3 & \\,0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341881 :question/math-academy-id "q-72860" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"}] :topic/title "Elementary Row Operations"}
